title: "Tackle: ACP v2 Server Support"
status: draft
created: 2026-10-23
author: adrian
context: "Tackle ships an ACP v1 server: the crate pins agent-client-protocol 2.2.0 and implements the stable v1 surface plus RFD-shaped fork, compaction, and notices gated behind client-advertised unstable capabilities (adrs/2026-09-17-tackle). That specification recorded ACP v2 as a non-goal (version-negotiation boundary only); this ADR revisits that decision.\n\nACP v2 has been published as a Draft since 2026-07-20. It is a consolidation release, not a superset: initialize is restructured (required info, one capabilities object, object support markers, session-nested groups); authenticate and logout become auth/login and auth/logout; session/load and session/set_mode are removed (session/resume gains replayFrom, modes become config options); the prompt response acknowledges insertion with a messageId instead of ending the turn, with foreground state and stop reasons moving to state_update notifications; updates become ID-keyed upserts; diffs become structured file changes; and the entire client filesystem and terminal execution surface is removed. v1 remains the stable negotiated wire version and is explicitly left unchanged.\n\nZed leads v2 client support behind its acp-beta feature flag and falls back to v1 when negotiation fails; the broader client ecosystem speaks v1, and the migration guide expects v1-only peers to remain common for some time. On the agent side the ACP organisation's own codex-acp adapter already speaks v1 and unstable v2 from one process, negotiated per connection, while Goose implements v1 only by deliberate choice. Proprietary clients are out of scope for this analysis. The pinned SDK already contains the complete stable v2 schema and runtime behind the unstable_protocol_v2 feature (Agent.v2, V2Builder, V2ConnectionTo, typed handlers for every baseline method), and session/fork behind unstable_session_fork; no SDK work is required to implement the baseline.\n\nThe v2 removal of the client filesystem and terminal surface is the significant risk. v1 reflects the client's fs and terminal RPCs to the model as client.* tools; v2 deletes that surface and directs clients to expose file access and command execution as an MCP server passed on session/new or session/resume. No client is observed doing so: clients forward the user's own MCP servers, which is a different thing, so a v1 client moving to v2 loses its client.* tooling unless it also ships an editor-capabilities MCP server. Tackle already connects client-provided mcpServers (MCP server forwarding). MCP-over-ACP is a separate draft (mcp/message, type acp) that tunnels MCP inside the ACP connection and is not required here. Reviewing how each v2 client replaces the removed surface is therefore a deliverable, not an afterthought.\n\nDocumentation lives under docs/docs/dev/tackle: protocol.mdx describes the wire surface and clients/ holds per-client notes (zed.mdx today).\n\nTackle is now fully ACP v1 conformant against the pinned acp-tck suite: the runner (crates/agentkit-tackle/mod.just) drives the binary over stdio through a deterministic test model, diffs the report against a committed baseline, and runs in CI (.github/workflows/tackle.conformance.yaml). The MANDATORY baseline is empty; the cancellation and agent-information gaps were closed in adrs/2026-10-24-tackle-turn-cancellation. acp-tck also carries an opt-in v2 suite (--protocol-version 2) covering the v2 baseline, so it is the natural validation instrument for this ADR, and v2 conformance precedes any manual testing.\n\n{{mermaid: architecture.mmd}}"
problem: "Tackle serves ACP v1 only. Clients that request v2 negotiate down to v1 or disconnect, and Tackle cannot use v2's redesigned lifecycle. No per-version account of supported functionality exists, and the v2 removal of the client filesystem and terminal surface threatens workflows that depend on client.* tools without a documented replacement path."
goals[7]:
  - "Serve ACP v2 alongside v1 with per-connection version negotiation"
  - "Reach feature parity with the v1 surface wherever v2 permits, and document where it cannot"
  - "Preserve the existing v1 server unchanged for v1 clients"
  - "Share protocol-neutral orchestration through an acp::common module that v1 and v2 may call but which cannot depend on either"
  - "Review ACP v2 client implementations for how they replace the removed filesystem and terminal surface, and document per client"
  - "Publish a per-version support matrix"
  - "Reach acp-tck v2 conformance before any manual or client testing"
nonGoals[7]:
  - "Draft v2 surfaces other than session/fork: subagents, plan operations, configurable LLM providers, MCP-over-ACP, client-provided system prompts, end-turn token usage"
  - "Per-client behavioural quirks beyond the documented support matrix"
  - "Dropping or deprecating ACP v1"
  - "Bumping the ACP SDK beyond 2.2.0"
  - "Re-architecting the protocol-neutral core (agent, store, invokables, mcp)"
  - "An MCP-over-ACP transport"
  - "Shipping an MCP server that re-exposes client editor capabilities, which is the client's responsibility under v2"
requirements[25]{id,title,slug,description}:
  FR-001,ACP v2 Server,acp-v2-server,"Tackle implements the ACP v2 stable baseline alongside v1. A single process serves both; the negotiated protocol version selects the surface per connection. The v2 surface covers initialize, auth/login and auth/logout, session/new, session/list, session/resume, session/close, session/delete, session/prompt, session/cancel, session/update, session/request_permission, session/set_config_option, and elicitation/create and elicitation/complete."
  FR-002,Version Negotiation,version-negotiation,"initialize selects the highest protocol version Tackle supports that is less than or equal to the client's requested version. A client requesting 2 is served v2; a client requesting 1 is served v1; a client requesting a version Tackle cannot satisfy is rejected with -32022 carrying requested and supported. A single connection speaks exactly one version after initialize."
  FR-003,v2 Initialization,v2-initialization,"The v2 initialize response carries the selected protocolVersion, required info (name and version), a single capabilities object with session-nested groups and object support markers, and authMethods. Tackle advertises session with prompt image and embeddedContext, mcp stdio and http, delete, and the draft session.fork marker."
  FR-004,v2 Authentication,v2-authentication,"Tackle implements auth/login and auth/logout, required because it advertises an authMethods entry. auth/login acknowledges the client's credential setup out-of-band exactly as v1 authenticate does, and auth/logout reports that no logged-in state is held. The v1 authenticate method is not served on a v2 connection."
  FR-005,v2 Session Lifecycle,v2-session-lifecycle,"session/new, session/list, session/resume, session/close, and session/delete behave as in v1 against the same store and ownership leases. session/new and session/resume accept optional mcpServers, cwd, and additionalDirectories. v1 session/load has no v2 counterpart: full-history reattachment is session/resume with replayFrom start. v2 responses carry configOptions and availableCommands, never modes."
  FR-006,v2 Prompt Lifecycle,v2-prompt-lifecycle,"session/prompt inserts the user message and responds with its messageId without waiting for the turn to finish. Foreground state is reported through state_update notifications: running when work starts, requires_action when blocked on the user, and idle with a stopReason when it ends. The v1 stop reasons map across, and v2 adds error, reported as an idle state_update carrying the failure."
  FR-007,v2 Message Updates,v2-message-updates,"Agent and thought output is streamed as agent_message_chunk and agent_thought_chunk carrying a required messageId. Whole-message upserts (user_message, agent_message, agent_thought) carry the full content array with omitted-unchanged, null-cleared, and value-replaced patch semantics; chunks append."
  FR-008,v2 Tool Calls,v2-tool-calls,"Tool calls are reported as tool_call_update upserts keyed by toolCallId; the first update creates the call. Streaming content uses tool_call_content_chunk. Agent-owned display terminals, where applicable, use terminal_update and terminal_output_chunk. Tackle emits no v1 diff content today, so the v2 structured-file-change shape is not exercised."
  FR-009,v2 Permission Requests,v2-permission-requests,"session/request_permission carries a required title and an optional subject (tool_call or command). The permission pipeline's ask fallback and MCP elicitation forwarding behave as in v1, and the session reports requires_action while foreground work is blocked and running when it resumes."
  FR-010,v2 Session Config Options,v2-config-options,"The model and actor selectors are exposed as v2 configOptions with a configId and a required type discriminator, changed with session/set_config_option, whose response carries the full updated list. Agent-initiated changes arrive as config_option_update. v1 modes are not used."
  FR-011,v2 Compaction,v2-compaction,"Context compaction is reported as a v2 compaction_update entity with a required compactionId and status, optionally streaming the retained summary through compaction_summary_chunk. v2 requires no client capability for compaction; the v1 RFD-gated path is retained only for v1 clients."
  FR-012,v2 Notices,v2-notices,"Script-driven model calls and other live user-facing events are reported as v2 notice updates with a required severity and title. v2 requires no client capability for notices; the v1 unstable-notices path is retained only for v1 clients."
  FR-013,v2 Usage Reporting,v2-usage,"usage_update carries used and size, and the cumulative session cost when knowable, exactly as in v1. Snapshots are sent after each model request and after each session lifecycle path."
  FR-014,v2 Slash Commands,v2-slash-commands,"available_commands_update advertises the command list with the v2 command input type discriminator. The slash-command intercepts (/fork, /!, /mcp, /editor, /context, compaction) are dispatched identically to v1 through the shared acp::common layer. The shared decision only classifies the prompt; each version must implement the execution and rendering of every intercept, and an unported intercept must fail explicitly rather than fall through to the model."
  FR-015,v2 Session Replay,v2-session-replay,"session/resume with replayFrom start replays the full user-visible history as session/update notifications before the resume response, using stable message, tool-call, and plan ids. A current-state snapshot follows replay and precedes the response. Retained user messages preserve the messageId returned by session/prompt."
  FR-016,v2 Session Fork,v2-session-fork,"For parity with the v1 fork fallback, Tackle implements the draft session/fork surface, advertised under the session.fork capability. A fork created this way is seeded, titled from its parent, and reported to the client; the built-in /fork prompt remains the fallback for clients that do not call session/fork. This is the only draft v2 surface in scope."
  FR-017,v2 MCP Integration,v2-mcp,"Tackle connects MCP servers from client-provided mcpServers, user configuration, and trust-gated project configuration as in v1, advertising session.mcp stdio and http. Because v2 removes the client filesystem and terminal surface, client-provided MCP servers are the only supported path by which a v2 client offers file access and command execution to the model, and no v2 client is known to provide one yet. MCP elicitation forwarding is unchanged."
  FR-018,v2 Elicitation,v2-elicitation,"elicitation/create is served when the client advertises a supported elicitation mode, and elicitation/complete is accepted. Elicitations never write the grant store, matching v1."
  FR-019,Client Capability Review,client-capability-review,"Tackle reviews the ACP v2 client implementations to determine how each replaces the removed client filesystem and terminal surface, whether by forwarding an MCP server, by shipping one, or not at all, and what that means for the workflows client.* tools support today. The review is a deliverable, recorded in the client documentation and in research/acp-v2-ecosystem.md. Its primary question is whether any client exposes editor capabilities as an MCP server, the replacement v2 directs clients to and which none is known to provide today."
  FR-020,Per-Client Documentation,per-client-documentation,"docs/docs/dev/tackle/clients/ gains a page per reviewed ACP v2 client recording its advertised capabilities, whether it negotiates v2, how it exposes file and command tooling under v2, and any behaviour Tackle must account for. The existing zed.mdx is extended and further clients are added."
  FR-021,Per-Version Support Matrix,per-version-support-matrix,"A support matrix in the Tackle documentation states, for each functionality area, whether it is supported under ACP v1 and under ACP v2, and marks draft-gated functionality as such. The matrix is the authoritative account of the parity gaps, including the client filesystem and terminal tools."
  FR-022,v1 Preservation,v1-preservation,"The existing ACP v1 server is preserved. v1 clients observe no behavioural change: the same capabilities, lifecycle, prompt semantics, and RFD-gated updates. v1 and v2 are selected per connection and never mixed."
  FR-023,Shared Core Boundary,shared-core-boundary,"Protocol-neutral orchestration is factored into acp::common and called by both acp::v1 and acp::v2. The two version modules must not depend on each other. Wire rendering, handler registration, and version-specific types stay in their version module."
  FR-024,Conformance Gate,v2-conformance-gate,"The v2 implementation is validated by the pinned acp-tck suite run with protocol version 2. Before any manual or client testing, acp-tck --protocol-version 2 against the tackle binary reports CONFORMANT: no MANDATORY failure, no untested MANDATORY requirement, no advertised-capability failure, and no version-mismatch or auth block. The conformance run is the primary acceptance signal for the v2 surface, and manual testing begins only once it passes."
  FR-025,Conformance Runner Coverage,conformance-runner-coverage,"The conformance runner (crates/agentkit-tackle/mod.just) runs both the v1 and v2 suites. The run is parameterised by protocol version, and each version has its own committed baseline so a v2 gap cannot hide behind the empty v1 baseline and vice versa. The v2 run negotiates protocol version 2 with the agent under test and fails on a version mismatch rather than reporting a pass."
nonFunctional[6]{id,title,slug,description}:
  NFR-001,Module Boundary Enforcement,module-boundary-enforcement,"The rule that acp::v1 and acp::v2 may depend on acp::common but not on each other is enforced in CI by arch-lint (arch-lint.toml), so a violation fails the build rather than relying on review."
  NFR-002,Protocol-Neutral Core,protocol-neutral-core,"The core (agent turn loop, store, invokables, mcp) remains free of ACP wire types. Version-specific behaviour is confined to the acp layer, reached through the existing TurnSink and related traits."
  NFR-003,Testability,testability,"Both surfaces are covered by the existing in-process ACP test harness. v2 tests exercise the new lifecycle (insertion response, state_update, upserts), and the harness includes a v2 client that forwards an MCP server so file and command tooling can be exercised under v2."
  NFR-004,Protocol Conformance,protocol-conformance,"Each connection strictly follows its negotiated version; v2 behaviour is not leaked onto v1 connections and vice versa. Conformance is verified by the pinned acp-tck suite for both protocol versions: the v1 suite must remain CONFORMANT with an empty MANDATORY baseline across the refactor, and the v2 suite must reach CONFORMANT before manual or client testing. The in-repo golden-transcript suite remains for behaviours the TCK does not cover."
  NFR-005,Unconditional Delivery,unconditional-delivery,"v2 is compiled and offered unconditionally, with no compile-time or runtime feature gate. The ACP SDK's unstable_protocol_v2 and unstable_session_fork features are enabled in the crate."
  NFR-006,Dependency Posture,dependency-posture,"Tackle stays on agent-client-protocol 2.2.0, whose v2 schema and runtime already implement the baseline. The only new dependency is arch-lint, a development-time CI check. No SDK bump, fork, or vendored patch."
acceptance[13]{id,title,slug,description}:
  AC-001,v1 Unaffected,v1-unaffected,"A v1 client completes a session lifecycle and a prompt turn exactly as before, receiving the same notifications and stop reasons."
  AC-002,v2 Turn,v2-turn,"A v2 client completes a prompt turn: session/prompt returns a messageId, the user and agent messages arrive as updates, foreground state moves running to idle with end_turn, and tool calls appear as tool_call_update upserts."
  AC-003,Negotiation,negotiation-works,"A client requesting v2 is served v2; one requesting v1 is served v1; one requesting an unsupported version is rejected with -32022 carrying requested and supported."
  AC-004,v2 Replay,v2-replay-works,"session/resume with replayFrom start replays the full user-visible history with stable ids and then reports current state before responding."
  AC-005,v2 Fork,v2-fork-works,"A v2 client calling session/fork receives a seeded fork; a v2 client without the capability can still create one through the /fork prompt."
  AC-006,Client Tooling via MCP,client-tooling-via-mcp,"A v2 client that forwards an MCP server exposing file and command tools can have the model invoke them, with the calls passing through the permission pipeline."
  AC-007,Support Matrix,support-matrix-present,"The documentation contains a per-version support matrix naming every functionality area and its v1 and v2 support, including the documented parity gaps."
  AC-008,Client Review,client-review-present,"The client documentation contains a reviewed page per ACP v2 client stating how it exposes file and command tooling under v2."
  AC-009,Boundary Enforced,boundary-enforced,"An import from acp::v1 into acp::v2 (or the reverse) fails the arch-lint check."
  AC-010,Cancellation,cancellation-works,"A v2 client cancelling a turn receives an idle state_update with the cancelled stop reason and no error."
  AC-011,v2 Conformance,v2-conformance,"acp-tck run with protocol version 2 against the tackle binary reports CONFORMANT: no MANDATORY failure or untested requirement, no advertised-capability failure, and no version-mismatch or auth block."
  AC-012,v1 Conformance Preserved,v1-conformance-preserved,"After the refactor and the v2 work, acp-tck run with protocol version 1 still reports CONFORMANT with an empty MANDATORY baseline."
  AC-013,Dispatch Parity,dispatch-parity,"Every Dispatch variant (Model, Fork, Direct, RuntimeCommand, Context, Compaction) is implemented under both v1 and v2. /! executes without a model request, /context and /mcp behave as in v1, /fork remains the fallback, and no variant falls through to the model. Each variant has a v2 integration test, and a new Dispatch variant fails compilation until both versions handle it."
edgeCases[9]{id,title,slug,description}:
  EC-001,Unsupported Version,unsupported-version,"A client requesting a protocol version Tackle does not implement is rejected with -32022 and the connection is not initialized; a client requesting a higher major version is not silently downgraded."
  EC-002,No MCP Servers,v2-no-mcp-servers,"A v2 client that forwards no MCP servers and expects file or command tooling has no such tools advertised; the absence is reported in the first-turn status rather than failing the session."
  EC-003,Unknown Enum Variants,unknown-v2-variants,"A v2 client sends or expects an unknown update, stop reason, or status value; Tackle treats it per the extensibility rules rather than failing the connection."
  EC-004,Cancel Before Insertion,cancel-before-insertion,"A session/cancel arriving before the user message is inserted answers the pending session/prompt with -32800 and emits no user-message update."
  EC-005,Failure After Insertion,failure-after-insertion,"A turn that fails after insertion reports an idle state_update with the error stop reason and the failure, rather than an error response to session/prompt."
  EC-006,Draft Fork Unavailable,draft-fork-unavailable,"A v2 client that does not call session/fork and does not use the /fork prompt creates no fork; nothing regresses relative to v1."
  EC-007,No Elicitation Support,v2-no-elicitation,"A v2 client advertising no elicitation mode is never sent elicitation/create; MCP elicitation falls back as in v1."
  EC-008,Replay Across Restart,replay-across-restart,"A session resumed from a restarted process replays the same user-visible history and reports a current-state snapshot; agent and thought ids are not assumed stable."
  EC-009,Client Tooling Absent,client-tooling-absent,"A v2 client that had client.* file and command tooling under v1 finds no equivalent under v2 unless it forwards an editor-capabilities MCP server, which no client is known to do; the capability is simply absent and the absence is surfaced in the first-turn status rather than failing the session."
